A||||20||DR650SE||Fuel injector kill switch, Help save a fiero!||||6||
Z||000000||DR650SE||10-29-2001||03:35 PM||||After reading DarkLord's terrible story, I have decided to wire a fuel injector kill switch.  I have a trunk release/rear defogger panel that I will install.  Instead of a defogger it will cut the power to the fuel injector fuses.  can anyone tell me how to go about this please help and save my Fiero.  I don't want it stolen.  Thanks for the help.<P>------------------<BR>2 Black 86 SE's V-6 4-SPD||||reg||6
Z||000001||DaRkLoRD||10-29-2001||04:51 PM||||Wouldn't have helped in my case.. they just picked it up and loaded it onto a trailer.. <P>good idea though!<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||9
Z||000002||falconhulk||10-29-2001||05:00 PM||||You have the plan.  You need a switch and some wire.  You can cut one side of the wire for the fuel pump fuse and wire the switch in line to this.  so that the switch has to be on for current to flow to the pump.  This is actually the basis for many alarm kill switches.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fiero_24||10-29-2001||05:06 PM||||I have thought of this and the way I am going to do it is by putting 2 switch's in.  one under the dash (where it cannot be hit on accident), and one in the ash tray area.  All you would have to do is tap into the injector wiring, but I am not sure of the effects on doing this.  I would not use the defroster switch, it is a momentary switch.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/driverside41.jpg"> <BR>  <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24</A> <BR>1987 GT||||reg||3
Z||000004||DR650SE||10-29-2001||06:32 PM||||SO can the defroster switch be used to make a kill switch ? The defroster has to be able to stay on till the switch is hit tright? so who do I make this work?<P>------------------<BR>2 Black 86 SE's V-6 4-SPD||||reg||6
Z||000005||theogre||10-29-2001||07:02 PM||||The defroster is a momentary switch with a remote timer/control box. Depending on which box is installed it runs:<P>10 minutes first activation 5 minutes each time after that.<P>5 minutes first activation 2.5 minutes each time after that.<P>That will continue untill the car is shut off.<P>Also, to intercept injectors on V6 you need a double pole switch.<P>Would probably be easier to intercept the fuel pump wiring. make sure you use a heavy switch. the fuel pump draws 6-7 amps. It's fused for 10 so for safety your switch needs to match or better yet exceed the fuse rating.<P>You can also intercept the wire for the starter solinoid. This is what most alarms do. You want a tap far down the wire... not right by the ignition switch. Otherwise it could be bypassed easily.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000006||DR650SE||10-29-2001||07:15 PM||||Thanks for the info. <P>------------------<BR>2 Black 86 SE's V-6 4-SPD||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fiero_24||10-29-2001||07:20 PM||||Good point theogre!  I never thought of shutting off the fuel pump.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/driverside41.jpg"> <BR>  <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24</A> <BR>1987 GT||||reg||14
Z||000008||88formula||10-29-2001||07:46 PM||||I just pull the 10-amp ECM fuse and stick it under the rug. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||FieroBUZZ||10-29-2001||08:32 PM||||Find a spot with back issues of 'Hot Rod Builder' magazine.  They recently had a little how-to on just such a 'kill' system for the fuel pump.  Every issue has at least one electronic or other neat do-dad article.<BR>Gary||||reg||1
Z||000010||DR650SE||10-29-2001||10:46 PM||||Just put one in.  I cut the fuel pump wire that goes to the fuse box.  Attached the ends of the wires to two terminals on a house hold light switch and put it up under the dash behind the fuse box.  Its hidden really well and I feel much safe with my ride now.  It took like 5-10 min.<P>------------------<BR>2 Black 86 SE's V-6 4-SPD||||reg||1
Z||000011||watts||10-30-2001||01:55 AM||||You can use the rear defrost switch to make a kill switch for the fuel pump.  You'll have to excuse the crappy drawing here (I'm no artist!  I admit it! ), but you'll get the idea.  Single relay, few bits of wire, and the stock switch - you're done!<P><A HREF="http://24.207.48.134/photos/latchingrelay.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://24.207.48.134/photos/latchingrelay.jpg</A> <P>(how the hell do I upload them so they are displayed here?!)<BR><p>[This message has been edited by watts (edited 10-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||falconhulk||10-30-2001||07:43 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fiero_24:<BR><B>Good point theogre!  I never thought of shutting off the fuel pump.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>ahemmmmm read my much earlier post!  lol! The fuel pump is what most alarm kill switches use.  I have a neon that I wired with an alarm years ago and it came with an adaper that you put in place for the fuel pump fuse and it went to the alarm box.  When you hit the remote the box closed an internal relay that put the new fuse, now contained in the box into the circuit.  This completed the circuit and allowed the car to start.  Confused more then 1 mechanic with this.  Its very simple and ver effective to your average kid car theif that does not understand the iginiton system.||||reg||1
Z||000013||cid_colada||10-30-2001||11:18 PM||||<BR>  JC Whitney sells a switch that just kills the positive lead from the battery, but allows enough juice to flow that your clock does'nt need reset every time you drive.  This switch comes with two keys and easily installs under the dash, out of sight.  They sell a few different models of these so you can take your pick.  This way seems easier to me.<P><BR>Black 84 SE||||reg||1
Z||000014||dennis_6||10-30-2001||11:30 PM||||At ITT one of our proffesors told us that he had a friend that had an electric fence charger hooked to his frame.  I do not recommend doing this, but it would be funny to see a car thief get ahold of that one. ||||reg||1
Z||000015||DR650SE||10-30-2001||11:42 PM||||Thanks for all the infor people!<P>------------------<BR>2 Black 86 SE's V-6 4-SPD||||reg||1
Z||000016||Mach10||10-30-2001||11:44 PM||||I'd like one that drops lock-pins into the door latches, rolls up the window, and fills the car with pepperspray/mace/CS tear gas...<P>As for the best one I've ever heard, a big bulk pack of 24Ga BARBED fishhooks, epoxied with care at strategic points under the dash. They found the poor bugger SCREAMING to be let out, one hand jammed semi-permanently under the dash... <P>"Geez officer, I don't know how THAT happened... Musta caught hisself onna jagged piece of metal..."  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_damon.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000017||GarethWright||10-31-2001||03:40 AM||||My last car was an MG Midget, and as they have the soft tops, the "How to stop car thieves" topic came up on Forum fairly often,  We decided that the Easiest course of action was to take the Distributer Rotor out whenever leaving the car somewhere, One guy (after getting his stolen 3 times and returned) rewired the entire under-dash harness with black wires.  Also a problem was getting Items stolen from inside the car, our solution was to gift-wrap some none-stinky trash and leave it on the passengers floor, figuring any thief would take the "gift" instead of rooting around for better items.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000018||roadwarrior||10-31-2001||12:07 PM||||have you guys ever thought of just hiding a double pole switch somewhere and cutting the power from the starter?<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by falconhulk:<BR><B> ahemmmmm read my much earlier post!  lol! The fuel pump is what most alarm kill switches use.  I have a neon that I wired with an alarm years ago and it came with an adaper that you put in place for the fuel pump fuse and it went to the alarm box.  When you hit the remote the box closed an internal relay that put the new fuse, now contained in the box into the circuit.  This completed the circuit and allowed the car to start.  Confused more then 1 mechanic with this.  Its very simple and ver effective to your average kid car theif that does not understand the iginiton system.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000019||roadwarrior||10-31-2001||12:09 PM||||Ever thought about trying to just not leave valuable stuff in your car?<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by GarethWright:<BR><B>My last car was an MG Midget, and as they have the soft tops, the "How to stop car thieves" topic came up on Forum fairly often,  We decided that the Easiest course of action was to take the Distributer Rotor out whenever leaving the car somewhere, One guy (after getting his stolen 3 times and returned) rewired the entire under-dash harness with black wires.  Also a problem was getting Items stolen from inside the car, our solution was to gift-wrap some none-stinky trash and leave it on the passengers floor, figuring any thief would take the "gift" instead of rooting around for better items.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000020||LZeitgeist||10-31-2001||06:20 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>As for the best one I've ever heard, a big bulk pack of 24Ga BARBED fishhooks, epoxied with care at strategic points under the dash. They found the poor bugger SCREAMING to be let out, one hand jammed semi-permanently under the dash...<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Ohhhhh... wicked... &lt;eg&gt;<P>Of course, that would get blood all over my near-mint OEM carpet and floormats... :\<P>I like the 'all black wires' trick.. that's pretty cool, although a pain in the butt come time to troubleshoot something...||||reg||1
